  "slug": "learning-body-interaction-fa56",
  "summary": "Four recent preprints extend generative trajectory models to division, mass change, and perturbation response in single-cell and spatial data.",
  "synthesis": "The shared thread is learning generative dynamics that transport cell states over time, using flow matching or Schrödinger bridges as the core machinery. ST-FateFlow couples gene-expression and spatial velocity fields with a time-conditioned differentiation potential learned from time-resolved spatial transcriptomics. HORIZON applies flow matching to spatiotemporal cell dynamics and removes rigid frame motion before modeling. Elysium is a simulation-free unbalanced Schrödinger bridge solver that jointly learns state transitions and mass change. MitoWorld generates single-cell future images under gene knockdown by combining latent dynamics, a division hazard, and diffusion rendering. What is new across the set is explicit treatment of division, mass change, and perturbation response within generative trajectory frameworks.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-23"
}
